The HTML-like tags in the Javadoc (`</ul>`, `<li>`, etc.) are actually standard Javadoc formatting tags. They're not HTML tags, but rather Javadoc's own documentation markup language that happens to look similar to HTML. Here's why they're used:

1. `<ul>` and `</ul>`: Creates an unordered list (bullet points)
2. `<li>`: Creates a list item within the unordered list
3. `<br>`: Creates a line break
4. `<b>`: Makes text bold
5. `<h2>`: Creates a section header

These tags are processed by the Javadoc tool when generating documentation, and they help create well-formatted, readable documentation. For example:

```java
/**
 * <ul>
 *   <li>First point</li>
 *   <li>Second point</li>
 * </ul>
 */
```

